{
  "ID": "9.3",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "Compare the differential genes on global proteome level and glycoproteome level for IPMN vs ND.  Provide a pathway level summary of biological functions of upregulated or downregulated glycopeptides whose global protein expression remained unchanged. ",
  "mode": "Baseline",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-22 12:39:00",
  "elapsed_seconds": 264.91,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nThis study compares proteome and glycoproteome profiles of intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMN) versus normal duct (ND) tissues. The analysis leverages global proteomics (Table S2A) and glycoproteomics (Table S3I) data from 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs, 5 IOPNs), 76 NDs, and 32 cyst fluid samples. The goal is to identify glycopeptide-level changes unaccompanied by global protein expression shifts, highlighting post-translational regulation in IPMN pathogenesis.  \n\n**Keywords**  \nIPMN, ND, glycoproteome, proteome, differential expression, post-translational modification, pathway enrichment  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nCPD, DDAH1, ERLIN2, TSPAN13, TSPAN15, GUSB, BSG, FAM3B, GNPTAB, FAM171A1, CNNM4  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \nThe provided datasets do not include pathway annotation or enrichment analysis outputs. However, the filtered glycopeptide-specific changes (n=27) suggest dysregulation of proteins involved in glycosylation, cell adhesion, and extracellular matrix remodeling (e.g., TSPAN13, BSG), though direct pathway-level conclusions cannot be drawn without additional functional annotations.  \n\n**Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset**  \nProteins with significant glycopeptide changes but stable global expression include:  \n- **CPD (carboxypeptidase D)**: Upregulated glycopeptides (log2FC 0.96–2.51), implicated in pro-hormone processing and tumor progression.  \n- **TSPAN13/15**: Upregulated glycopeptides (log2FC 1.07–2.44), linked to cell-cell adhesion and Wnt signaling.  \n- **BSG (Basigin)**: Upregulated glycopeptide (log2FC 2.25), involved in matrix metalloproteinase induction.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)**  \n| Gene    | Direction | Role/Function                          | Pathway(s)                     |  \n|---------|-----------|----------------------------------------|--------------------------------|  \n| **CPD** | Up        | Pro-hormone processing, tumor growth   | Glycoprotein processing        |  \n| **TSPAN13** | Up     | Cell adhesion, Wnt signaling           | Cell-cell communication        |  \n| **BSG** | Up        | Matrix remodeling, MMP induction       | Extracellular matrix (ECM)     |  \n\n**Comparisons**  \nGlycopeptide-specific changes (e.g., CPD, TSPAN13) highlight post-translational regulation distinct from global proteome shifts. Discordant findings (e.g., upregulated glycopeptides without global changes) suggest context-specific glycosylation as a potential driver in IPMN.  \n\n**Implications**  \nUpregulated glycopeptides in CPD, TSPAN13, and BSG implicate altered glycosylation and ECM interactions in IPMN biology, potentially offering novel biomarkers or therapeutic targets.  \n\n**Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. **Pathway validation**: Integrate external pathway databases (e.g., KEGG, Reactome) to annotate the 27 glycopeptide-specific proteins.  \n2. **Functional assays**: Validate the role of CPD and TSPAN13 glycosylation in IPMN cell models.  \n3. **Cyst fluid correlation**: Compare glycopeptide profiles in tissue vs. cyst fluid to assess clinical utility.  \n4. **IOPN analysis**: Extend comparisons to IOPNs (n=5) to evaluate subtype-specific patterns.  \n\n**References**  \nNone validated in this analysis due to lack of pathway annotation in provided datasets.  \n\n---  \n**Note**: Pathway-level conclusions are limited by the absence of functional annotations in the input data. Further analysis requires integration with pathway databases or gene ontology resources.",
